ping more pieces can lead to better board control.\n\nLet's analyze G4 and H5.\n\nFirst, G4 (G,4):\n\nFlips G5, G6 (right direction) and F5 (up-right).\n\nAfter flipping:\n\n- G5 and G6 become @.\n\n- F5 becomes @.\n\nThis strengthens the lower part of the board. Also, G4 is in row G, column4. Not a corner, but maybe helps in controlling the center.\n\nH5 (H,5):\n\nFlips G5, F5, E5 (up direction).\n\nAfter flipping:\n\n- G5, F5, E5 become @.\n\nThis is a vertical line in column5. E5 was a *, now becomes @. This might help in taking control of column5, which has several *s.\n\nBut what about the implications for future moves?\n\nLet's consider the board after each move.\n\nFor G4:\n\nAfter placing @ at G4, flipping G5, G6, F5.\n\nSo:\n\n- G5 and G6 were *, now @.\n\n- F5 was *, now @.\n\nThis would make row G: . . . @ @ @ @ . (since G4 is @, G5 and G6 flipped to @, G7 is already @).\n\nWait, original G row: . . . . * * @ . → after flipping G5 and G6, it becomes . . . @ @ @ @ . (G4, G5, G6, G7 all @).\n\nThis creates a solid line on row G from G4 to G7. That's strong, as it's hard to flip those later.\n\nAlso, flipping F5 to @ might open up more opportunities in column5.\n\nFor H5:\n\nAfter placing @ at H5, flipping G5, F5, E5.\n\nColumn5 becomes: A5 (*), B5 (@), C5 (@), D5 (@), E5 (@), F5 (@), G5 (@), H5 (@).\n\nSo column5 is almost all @, except A5. This could be very powerful, as it's a full column except the top.\n\nHowever, A5 is still *, but maybe can be flipped later.\n\nBut H5 is on the edge (row H), which is a risky position because edge positions can sometimes be vulnerable, but in this case, since it's the bottom row, maybe it's safer.\n\nWait, in Reversi, edges can be good if you can secure them, but corners are best. H5 is not a corner.\n\nComparing the two:\n\nG4 is in row G, which is second from bottom. Securing row G could be good.\n\nH5 is in column5, securing column5.\n\nBut which move gives more long-term advantage?\n\nAnother consideration: mobilit